syntax: add a ksh93 language variant

ksh93 (ksh93u+m sh(1)) shares most syntax with mksh, but LangMirBSDKorn
rejects much of it, and parsing a ksh93 script as bash or mksh both fail
on its own forms:

	print -r -- ${.sh.version}          # variable names with dots
	point=(x=1 y=(z=2))                 # compound variables
	print ${point.y.z} ${a[1][2].name}  # members and further subscripts
	typeset -T Pt_t=(integer len=0; get() { :; })
	Pt_t p=(len=2) q                    # declaration commands made by types
	enum Color_t=(red green); Color_t c=red
	function .sh.math.hypot x y { .sh.value=$(( sqrt(x*x + y*y) )); }
	(( .sh.value = _.len++ ))           # variable names with dots in arithmetic
	print $(( hypot(3, 4) ))            # arithmetic function calls
	namespace geo { origin=0; }
	cat <<# EOF                         # strip leading blanks
	print x >; out                      # replace the file on success
	tr a-z A-Z <>; f; exec 3<#((EOF)) 3<#pat 3<##pat 3>#((CUR+10))
	print $(3<#)                        # the offset of a file descriptor
	print ${@x} ${#prefix@} ${!prefix*} ${!point.*}
	print @(a|b) ~(E)a.*                # pattern groups
	case $x in a) ;;& esac

Reproducer, before this change:

	$ printf 'print -r -- ${.sh.version}\n' | shfmt -ln mksh
	<standard input>:1:15: invalid parameter name
	$ printf 'point=(x=1 y=(z=2))\n' | shfmt -ln mksh
	<standard input>:1:14: array element values must be words
	$ printf 'function .sh.math.hypot x y { :; }\n' | shfmt -ln mksh
	<standard input>:1:1: multi-name functions are a zsh feature; tried parsing as mksh
	$ printf 'namespace geo { :; }\n' | shfmt -ln mksh
	<standard input>:1:20: `}` can only be used to close a block
	$ printf 'print $(( hypot(3, 4) ))\n' | shfmt -ln mksh
	<standard input>:1:16: not a valid arithmetic operator: `(`
	$ printf 'cat <<# EOF\n' | shfmt -ln mksh
	<standard input>:1:5: `<<` must be followed by a word
	$ printf 'print x >; out\n' | shfmt -ln mksh
	<standard input>:1:9: `>` must be followed by a word
	$ printf 'print ${@x}\n' | shfmt -ln mksh
	<standard input>:1:10: `@` cannot be followed by a word
	$ printf 'print ${#pre@}\n' | shfmt -ln mksh
	<standard input>:1:13: cannot combine multiple parameter expansion operators

LangKsh93 is a new variant, accepted by LangVariant.Set as "ksh93" or
"ksh". The features which mksh and ksh93 share are now checked against
both. ArrayExpr gains Stmts for compound assignments, which hold a list of
commands; ArrayElem and Assign gain Array for nested values; Assign and
ParamExp gain Elems for further subscripts and members; ParamExp gains
Attributes for ${@a}; ArithmExp gains Bare for the expression of a seek
redirection; FuncDecl gains Params for the arguments of an arithmetic
function; and the new NamespaceClause node represents a name space. A
command which assigns a compound or array value, such as Pt_t p=(len=2),
is a DeclClause, and so is any command named after a type which an
earlier enum or typeset -T defined in the same file. The new redirection
operators are HashHdoc, RdrOutReplace, RdrInOutTrunc, SeekIn, SeekInCopy
and SeekOut, with their tokens; a seek redirection without an operand has
a nil Word.

The ksh93 shell for TestParseConfirm is only used when `ksh --version`
reports ksh93u+m, and the expectations of the existing tests for mksh
also apply to ksh93 unless a test sets them separately.

token_string.go was regenerated with `go run
golang.org/x/tools/cmd/stringer@v0.52.0 -type token -linecomment
-trimprefix _`, and tokens_parse.go with `go run gen_token_parse.go`.

Upstream: https://github.com/mvdan/sh/issues/614

